<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-num-project-summary">
  <title>Data Set Summary and Research Questions</title>

  <p>
    Explain what your data represent and identify the questions you will
    investigate. Your reader should be able to understand the study
    without locating the original data source. This section should be
    <alert>no more than one page</alert>.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-summary-content">
    <title>Data Set Summary</title>
    <p>
      Write in complete sentences. Include the following information when
      it is available.
      <ol marker="1)">
        <li><p><alert>Context:</alert> Briefly explain the biological,
        health, or other scientific topic being studied.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Observational units:</alert> Explain what one
        observation represents. If the data contain repeated measurements,
        identify the individuals or matched pairs.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Population and sample:</alert> Identify who or what
        was observed and the population to which you hope the findings
        apply.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Sample sizes:</alert> State the number of usable
        observations for the one-mean analysis, the number in each
        independent group or the number of complete pairs for the
        two-mean analysis, and the number in each ANOVA group.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Data collection:</alert> Describe how the data were
        collected, including whether the study was observational or
        experimental and whether random sampling or random assignment
        was used, when known.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Variables:</alert> Identify the numerical response
        variables, their units, the group labels, and any pairing. For
        paired data, state the order used to calculate differences.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Data preparation:</alert> Describe filtering,
        missing values, unit conversions, and other changes that affect
        the observations included in the analyses.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
      If you use two data sets, summarize both and explain which analyses
      use each one. Refer to your source documentation rather than
      guessing about unavailable study details.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-research-questions">
    <title>Research Questions</title>
    <p>
      State three clear research questions using the actual variables,
      groups, and populations in your data.
      <ol marker="1)">
        <li>
          <p><alert>One population mean:</alert> Ask either an estimation
          question to answer with a one-mean <m>t</m> confidence interval,
          or a question comparing a population mean with a justified
          benchmark to answer with a one-mean <m>t</m> hypothesis test.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><alert>A comparison of two means:</alert> Ask whether there
          is evidence of a mean difference within paired measurements or
          between two independent groups. Explain which design applies.
          A directional question is appropriate when its direction is
          justified before examining the test results.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><alert>A comparison of three or more means:</alert> Ask
          whether the population mean of a numerical response differs
          among at least three independent groups. Identify the grouping
          variable and the groups to be compared using one-way ANOVA.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</subsection>
